1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,390
Sweetheart, at every Christmas dinner I've ever

2
00:00:03,390 --> 00:00:06,060
been to, the first thing to disappear

3
00:00:06,060 --> 00:00:08,080
isn't the ham or the turkey.

4
00:00:08,260 --> 00:00:09,781
It's the rolls.

5
00:00:09,961 --> 00:00:14,107
Soft, buttery, golden homemade dinner rolls that

6
00:00:14,107 --> 00:00:16,439
pull apart in your fingers.

7
00:00:16,619 --> 00:00:19,279
But most people are scared of yeast,

8
00:00:19,279 --> 00:00:21,200
so they buy a bag of rolls

9
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:24,229
from the store that taste like cardboard,

10
00:00:24,229 --> 00:00:25,780
or they try homemade,

11
00:00:25,780 --> 00:00:27,479
and they come out hard,

12
00:00:27,479 --> 00:00:29,843
flat, and dense as hockey pucks.

13
00:00:30,193 --> 00:00:32,906
So tonight I'm giving you my mother's

14
00:00:32,906 --> 00:00:34,593
Christmas dinner rolls,

15
00:00:34,593 --> 00:00:35,546
step by step,

16
00:00:35,546 --> 00:00:38,406
the soft, buttery folded kind they used

17
00:00:38,406 --> 00:00:40,386
to call Parker House rolls.

18
00:00:40,566 --> 00:00:43,179
And the five mistakes that make yeast

19
00:00:43,179 --> 00:00:43,956
rolls hard.

20
00:00:44,136 --> 00:00:45,023
Stay with me,

21
00:00:45,023 --> 00:00:47,684
because the last secret is something my

22
00:00:47,684 --> 00:00:49,935
mother did with a pastry brush in

23
00:00:49,935 --> 00:00:51,027
the last minute,

24
00:00:51,027 --> 00:00:53,347
and it's why her rolls were famous

25
00:00:53,347 --> 00:00:55,735
at First Methodist for forty years.

26
00:00:56,085 --> 00:00:59,247
My mother made rolls every Saturday of

27
00:00:59,247 --> 00:01:00,579
her life, honey,

28
00:01:00,579 --> 00:01:03,492
and three dozen extra at Christmas.

29
00:01:03,672 --> 00:01:06,415
She'd start the dough right after breakfast,

30
00:01:06,415 --> 00:01:08,659
and the whole house would smell like

31
00:01:08,659 --> 00:01:10,591
warm yeast and butter by lunch.

32
00:01:10,771 --> 00:01:13,315
Danny and I would hang around the

33
00:01:13,315 --> 00:01:15,473
kitchen like hungry puppies.

34
00:01:15,653 --> 00:01:17,739
She'd let us each have one hot

35
00:01:17,739 --> 00:01:19,407
roll from the first pan,

36
00:01:19,407 --> 00:01:21,354
with butter melting into it.

37
00:01:21,534 --> 00:01:23,556
That was worth the wait for the

38
00:01:23,556 --> 00:01:24,274
whole week.

39
00:01:24,624 --> 00:01:27,204
These rolls are named after the Parker

40
00:01:27,204 --> 00:01:28,698
House Hotel in Boston,

41
00:01:28,698 --> 00:01:31,007
where a baker invented them in the

42
00:01:31,007 --> 00:01:32,298
eighteen seventies.

43
00:01:32,478 --> 00:01:34,392
The story goes that he was in

44
00:01:34,392 --> 00:01:36,372
a bad mood one night and threw

45
00:01:36,372 --> 00:01:38,616
half-finished rolls into the oven,

46
00:01:38,616 --> 00:01:41,058
and they came out folded and perfect.

47
00:01:41,238 --> 00:01:43,407
I don't know if that's true,

48
00:01:43,407 --> 00:01:45,034
honey, but I like it.

49
00:01:45,214 --> 00:01:47,259
In a minute I'll tell you the

50
00:01:47,259 --> 00:01:50,433
one temperature mistake that kills more yeast

51
00:01:50,433 --> 00:01:51,843
rolls than anything.

52
00:01:52,194 --> 00:01:54,330
People think yeast is hard.

53
00:01:54,510 --> 00:01:56,542
It isn't, sweetheart.

54
00:01:56,722 --> 00:01:58,371
Yeast is just alive,

55
00:01:58,371 --> 00:01:59,856
like a little pet.

56
00:02:00,036 --> 00:02:01,861
It wants to be warm,

57
00:02:01,861 --> 00:02:02,590
not hot.

58
00:02:02,770 --> 00:02:04,895
It wants a little sugar to eat.

59
00:02:05,075 --> 00:02:06,712
And it wants time.

60
00:02:06,892 --> 00:02:09,473
If you give it those three things,

61
00:02:09,473 --> 00:02:11,523
it will do all the work for

62
00:02:11,523 --> 00:02:11,826
you.

63
00:02:12,006 --> 00:02:14,925
My mother never measured the temperature in

64
00:02:14,925 --> 00:02:15,536
her life.

65
00:02:15,716 --> 00:02:18,064
She tested the milk on her wrist,

66
00:02:18,064 --> 00:02:19,559
like a baby's bottle.

67
00:02:19,909 --> 00:02:22,371
Here's what you need for about two

68
00:02:22,371 --> 00:02:23,241
dozen rolls.

69
00:02:23,421 --> 00:02:25,116
One cup of whole milk.

70
00:02:25,296 --> 00:02:26,886
Half a cup of butter,

71
00:02:26,886 --> 00:02:28,628
plus more for brushing.

72
00:02:28,808 --> 00:02:30,851
A quarter cup of sugar.

73
00:02:31,031 --> 00:02:33,614
One packet of active dry yeast,

74
00:02:33,614 --> 00:02:36,697
which is two and a quarter teaspoons.

75
00:02:36,877 --> 00:02:39,210
A quarter cup of warm water.

76
00:02:39,390 --> 00:02:40,459
Two eggs.

77
00:02:40,639 --> 00:02:43,506
One and a half teaspoons of salt.

78
00:02:43,686 --> 00:02:46,972
And about four cups of all-purpose flour.

79
00:02:47,152 --> 00:02:48,139
That's all.

80
00:02:48,319 --> 00:02:50,826
Things you probably have right now.

81
00:02:51,177 --> 00:02:53,429
And here's mistake number one.

82
00:02:53,609 --> 00:02:54,932
Killing the yeast.

83
00:02:55,112 --> 00:02:57,683
Heat the milk and butter together just

84
00:02:57,683 --> 00:02:59,238
until the butter melts,

85
00:02:59,238 --> 00:03:01,470
then let it cool until it's warm,

86
00:03:01,470 --> 00:03:02,485
like bathwater.

87
00:03:02,665 --> 00:03:05,358
About a hundred and ten degrees.

88
00:03:05,538 --> 00:03:07,569
If it's too hot to hold your

89
00:03:07,569 --> 00:03:09,164
finger in comfortably,

90
00:03:09,164 --> 00:03:10,832
it's too hot for yeast.

91
00:03:11,012 --> 00:03:13,038
Hot milk kills the yeast,

92
00:03:13,038 --> 00:03:15,146
and your rolls never rise.

93
00:03:15,326 --> 00:03:18,495
That's the temperature mistake I promised you.

94
00:03:18,845 --> 00:03:20,826
Stir the yeast and a pinch of

95
00:03:20,826 --> 00:03:23,080
the sugar into the quarter cup of

96
00:03:23,080 --> 00:03:25,062
warm water and let it sit for

97
00:03:25,062 --> 00:03:25,950
five minutes.

98
00:03:26,130 --> 00:03:28,755
It should get foamy and smell like

99
00:03:28,755 --> 00:03:29,218
bread.

100
00:03:29,398 --> 00:03:31,581
That's how you know it's alive.

101
00:03:31,761 --> 00:03:34,061
If it just sits there flat,

102
00:03:34,061 --> 00:03:35,595
your yeast is old,

103
00:03:35,595 --> 00:03:36,532
sweetheart.

104
00:03:36,712 --> 00:03:38,643
Throw it out and buy a new

105
00:03:38,643 --> 00:03:39,162
packet.

106
00:03:39,342 --> 00:03:41,497
Better to lose a packet of yeast

107
00:03:41,497 --> 00:03:43,786
than a whole morning and four cups

108
00:03:43,786 --> 00:03:44,393
of flour.

109
00:03:44,743 --> 00:03:45,908
In a big bowl,

110
00:03:45,908 --> 00:03:48,324
mix the warm milk and butter,

111
00:03:48,324 --> 00:03:50,156
the rest of the sugar,

112
00:03:50,156 --> 00:03:51,738
the salt, the eggs,

113
00:03:51,738 --> 00:03:53,404
and the foamy yeast.

114
00:03:53,584 --> 00:03:55,785
Then add the flour one cup at

115
00:03:55,785 --> 00:03:58,594
a time, stirring with a wooden spoon,

116
00:03:58,594 --> 00:04:00,264
until you have a soft,

117
00:04:00,264 --> 00:04:03,073
shaggy dough that pulls away from the

118
00:04:03,073 --> 00:04:03,452
bowl.

119
00:04:03,632 --> 00:04:06,082
And here's mistake number two.

120
00:04:06,262 --> 00:04:08,026
Adding too much flour.

121
00:04:08,206 --> 00:04:12,131
Soft, slightly sticky dough makes soft rolls.

122
00:04:12,311 --> 00:04:14,284
Stiff dough makes hard ones.

123
00:04:14,635 --> 00:04:16,482
If this is helping you,

124
00:04:16,482 --> 00:04:18,650
tap the like button for me,

125
00:04:18,650 --> 00:04:19,534
sweetheart.

126
00:04:19,714 --> 00:04:22,431
It helps Grandma more than you know.

127
00:04:22,611 --> 00:04:24,575
Turn the dough out on a lightly

128
00:04:24,575 --> 00:04:26,983
floured counter and knead it for about

129
00:04:26,983 --> 00:04:27,870
eight minutes.

130
00:04:28,050 --> 00:04:30,547
Push it away with the heel of

131
00:04:30,547 --> 00:04:32,613
your hand, fold it back,

132
00:04:32,613 --> 00:04:34,679
turn it, and push again.

133
00:04:34,859 --> 00:04:36,189
When it's ready,

134
00:04:36,189 --> 00:04:38,515
it'll be smooth and springy,

135
00:04:38,515 --> 00:04:40,177
like a baby's cheek.

136
00:04:40,357 --> 00:04:43,374
Poke it, and it should slowly bounce

137
00:04:43,374 --> 00:04:43,793
back.

138
00:04:44,144 --> 00:04:46,164
And mistake number three.

139
00:04:46,344 --> 00:04:48,201
Not kneading long enough.

140
00:04:48,381 --> 00:04:50,762
Kneading builds up the stretch in the

141
00:04:50,762 --> 00:04:53,400
dough, which traps the little air bubbles

142
00:04:53,400 --> 00:04:54,430
the yeast makes.

143
00:04:54,610 --> 00:04:56,897
Five minutes isn't enough.

144
00:04:57,077 --> 00:04:58,633
Set a timer for eight.

145
00:04:58,813 --> 00:05:00,657
It's good exercise,

146
00:05:00,657 --> 00:05:01,239
honey.

147
00:05:01,419 --> 00:05:04,300
My mother said kneading bread was cheaper

148
00:05:04,300 --> 00:05:05,705
than a psychiatrist,

149
00:05:05,705 --> 00:05:06,969
and she was right.

150
00:05:07,149 --> 00:05:08,849
Whatever's bothering you,

151
00:05:08,849 --> 00:05:10,481
punch it into the dough.

152
00:05:10,831 --> 00:05:13,258
Put the dough in a buttered bowl,

153
00:05:13,258 --> 00:05:15,318
turn it over once so the top

154
00:05:15,318 --> 00:05:17,672
is buttered, and cover it with a

155
00:05:17,672 --> 00:05:18,923
clean dish towel.

156
00:05:19,103 --> 00:05:22,012
Let it rise somewhere warm until it's

157
00:05:22,012 --> 00:05:23,270
doubled in size,

158
00:05:23,270 --> 00:05:25,472
about an hour to an hour and

159
00:05:25,472 --> 00:05:26,022
a half.

160
00:05:26,202 --> 00:05:28,941
And mistake number four is rushing it,

161
00:05:28,941 --> 00:05:31,032
or putting it somewhere cold.

162
00:05:31,212 --> 00:05:34,017
A drafty kitchen in December can take

163
00:05:34,017 --> 00:05:34,927
three hours.

164
00:05:35,107 --> 00:05:36,338
Be patient.

165
00:05:36,518 --> 00:05:39,139
The rise is where the flavor comes

166
00:05:39,139 --> 00:05:39,525
from.

167
00:05:39,875 --> 00:05:42,505
Here's my mother's trick for a cold

168
00:05:42,505 --> 00:05:44,008
kitchen, sweetheart.

169
00:05:44,188 --> 00:05:46,193
Turn your oven on to the lowest

170
00:05:46,193 --> 00:05:48,069
setting for just two minutes,

171
00:05:48,069 --> 00:05:49,168
then turn it off.

172
00:05:49,348 --> 00:05:51,238
Put the bowl in the oven with

173
00:05:51,238 --> 00:05:53,389
the light on and the door closed.

174
00:05:53,569 --> 00:05:55,706
It's the perfect warm,

175
00:05:55,706 --> 00:05:57,261
draft-free spot.

176
00:05:57,441 --> 00:05:59,778
Just remember to take the bowl out

177
00:05:59,778 --> 00:06:01,841
before you preheat for baking.

178
00:06:02,021 --> 00:06:04,639
I melted a plastic bowl once,

179
00:06:04,639 --> 00:06:06,897
in nineteen seventy-nine.

180
00:06:07,077 --> 00:06:09,457
Harold still teased me about it.

181
00:06:09,807 --> 00:06:11,836
How do you know it's doubled,

182
00:06:11,836 --> 00:06:12,256
honey?

183
00:06:12,436 --> 00:06:14,445
Here's the finger test.

184
00:06:14,625 --> 00:06:17,204
Flour your finger and poke it gently

185
00:06:17,204 --> 00:06:18,279
into the dough,

186
00:06:18,279 --> 00:06:19,640
about half an inch.

187
00:06:19,820 --> 00:06:21,914
If the hole stays there,

188
00:06:21,914 --> 00:06:22,874
it's ready.

189
00:06:23,054 --> 00:06:26,025
If it springs right back and fills

190
00:06:26,025 --> 00:06:28,034
in, it needs more time.

191
00:06:28,214 --> 00:06:31,330
If the whole dough sighs and collapses,

192
00:06:31,330 --> 00:06:32,687
it went too long,

193
00:06:32,687 --> 00:06:33,965
but don't worry,

194
00:06:33,965 --> 00:06:35,483
just punch it down,

195
00:06:35,483 --> 00:06:38,199
shape it, and it'll still be fine.

196
00:06:38,549 --> 00:06:40,116
So far, warm milk,

197
00:06:40,116 --> 00:06:43,425
soft dough, eight minutes of kneading,

198
00:06:43,425 --> 00:06:44,818
and a good rise.

199
00:06:44,998 --> 00:06:47,251
Punch the dough down gently and roll

200
00:06:47,251 --> 00:06:49,253
it out on the counter about half

201
00:06:49,253 --> 00:06:50,130
an inch thick.

202
00:06:50,310 --> 00:06:52,777
Cut it into circles with a biscuit

203
00:06:52,777 --> 00:06:54,083
cutter or a glass.

204
00:06:54,263 --> 00:06:57,130
Brush each circle with melted butter.

205
00:06:57,310 --> 00:06:59,564
Then press the back of a butter

206
00:06:59,564 --> 00:07:01,308
knife across the middle,

207
00:07:01,308 --> 00:07:03,852
off-center, and fold each one over,

208
00:07:03,852 --> 00:07:05,670
like a little pocketbook.

209
00:07:06,020 --> 00:07:08,191
Line them up in a buttered nine

210
00:07:08,191 --> 00:07:09,312
by thirteen pan,

211
00:07:09,312 --> 00:07:12,184
close together, so they're just touching.

212
00:07:12,364 --> 00:07:14,489
That's important, honey.

213
00:07:14,669 --> 00:07:17,675
Rolls baked touching each other stay soft

214
00:07:17,675 --> 00:07:18,628
on the sides.

215
00:07:18,808 --> 00:07:22,152
Rolls baked apart get crusty all over.

216
00:07:22,332 --> 00:07:24,932
Cover them again with the towel and

217
00:07:24,932 --> 00:07:27,013
let them rise a second time,

218
00:07:27,013 --> 00:07:28,870
about forty-five minutes,

219
00:07:28,870 --> 00:07:31,694
until they're puffy and filling up the

220
00:07:31,694 --> 00:07:31,991
pan.

221
00:07:32,342 --> 00:07:34,603
And you can make them ahead for

222
00:07:34,603 --> 00:07:36,208
Christmas, sweetheart.

223
00:07:36,388 --> 00:07:39,494
After shaping, cover the pan with plastic

224
00:07:39,494 --> 00:07:41,691
wrap and put it in the icebox

225
00:07:41,691 --> 00:07:42,448
overnight.

226
00:07:42,628 --> 00:07:43,884
On Christmas Day,

227
00:07:43,884 --> 00:07:46,618
take it out three hours before dinner

228
00:07:46,618 --> 00:07:49,204
and let it come to room temperature

229
00:07:49,204 --> 00:07:50,534
and finish rising.

230
00:07:50,714 --> 00:07:51,748
Then bake.

231
00:07:51,928 --> 00:07:53,383
They're just as good,

232
00:07:53,383 --> 00:07:55,740
and the morning of Christmas is so

233
00:07:55,740 --> 00:07:56,572
much calmer.

234
00:07:56,922 --> 00:07:59,807
Bake at three hundred seventy-five for about

235
00:07:59,807 --> 00:08:01,643
fifteen to eighteen minutes,

236
00:08:01,643 --> 00:08:04,201
until they're deep golden brown on top.

237
00:08:04,381 --> 00:08:07,063
Mistake number five is underbaking.

238
00:08:07,243 --> 00:08:09,831
Pale rolls taste raw and doughy in

239
00:08:09,831 --> 00:08:10,668
the middle.

240
00:08:10,848 --> 00:08:12,441
You want real color,

241
00:08:12,441 --> 00:08:14,830
the color of a good paper bag.

242
00:08:15,010 --> 00:08:17,727
Now, the secret I promised you.

243
00:08:17,907 --> 00:08:20,117
The second they come out of the

244
00:08:20,117 --> 00:08:22,969
oven, brush the tops with melted butter.

245
00:08:23,149 --> 00:08:24,321
Generously.

246
00:08:24,672 --> 00:08:27,644
And then, my mother's last-minute trick.

247
00:08:27,824 --> 00:08:30,195
Sprinkle a few flakes of coarse salt

248
00:08:30,195 --> 00:08:31,643
over the buttery tops.

249
00:08:31,823 --> 00:08:32,938
Just a pinch.

250
00:08:33,118 --> 00:08:35,684
The salt against the sweet butter makes

251
00:08:35,684 --> 00:08:38,184
people close their eyes when they take

252
00:08:38,184 --> 00:08:38,644
a bite.

253
00:08:38,824 --> 00:08:41,040
Then cover the pan with a towel

254
00:08:41,040 --> 00:08:42,255
for five minutes,

255
00:08:42,255 --> 00:08:44,327
so the steam keeps them soft.

256
00:08:44,507 --> 00:08:47,436
The ladies at First Methodist begged her

257
00:08:47,436 --> 00:08:49,778
for that recipe for forty years.

258
00:08:49,958 --> 00:08:52,176
She never told them about the salt.

259
00:08:52,526 --> 00:08:55,096
And don't throw out the leftovers,

260
00:08:55,096 --> 00:08:55,927
sweetheart.

261
00:08:56,107 --> 00:08:59,310
Day-old rolls make the best little ham

262
00:08:59,310 --> 00:09:02,343
sandwiches on December twenty-sixth,

263
00:09:02,343 --> 00:09:03,860
split and toasted,

264
00:09:03,860 --> 00:09:05,883
with a smear of mustard.

265
00:09:06,063 --> 00:09:08,612
Or tear them up for bread pudding,

266
00:09:08,612 --> 00:09:11,462
or for the stuffing next Thanksgiving.

267
00:09:11,642 --> 00:09:14,402
They freeze beautifully for three months,

268
00:09:14,402 --> 00:09:14,672
too.

269
00:09:14,852 --> 00:09:17,143
Warm them wrapped in foil at three

270
00:09:17,143 --> 00:09:19,569
fifty for ten minutes and they taste

271
00:09:19,569 --> 00:09:20,378
fresh-baked.

272
00:09:20,558 --> 00:09:23,422
My mother never wasted one roll in

273
00:09:23,422 --> 00:09:24,180
her life.

274
00:09:24,531 --> 00:09:25,947
So one more time.

275
00:09:26,127 --> 00:09:28,414
Warm milk, not hot.

276
00:09:28,594 --> 00:09:31,357
Proof your yeast until it foams.

277
00:09:31,537 --> 00:09:33,186
Keep the dough soft.

278
00:09:33,366 --> 00:09:35,107
Knead for eight minutes.

279
00:09:35,287 --> 00:09:37,447
Let it double somewhere warm.

280
00:09:37,627 --> 00:09:40,461
Fold them like pocketbooks and bake them

281
00:09:40,461 --> 00:09:41,098
touching.

282
00:09:41,278 --> 00:09:43,891
Golden brown, not pale.

283
00:09:44,071 --> 00:09:46,138
And melted butter and a pinch of

284
00:09:46,138 --> 00:09:48,076
salt the second they come out.

285
00:09:48,256 --> 00:09:50,795
Those are the rolls everybody at your

286
00:09:50,795 --> 00:09:52,854
